From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Witold=20Bo=C5=82t?= <witold.bolt@jit.team>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 10:41:15 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] broadcom/compiler: move every channel of a vector mov

nir_trivialize_registers copies whole registers with vector movs: before
a store_reg whose value is not trivially stored (e.g. a load_reg, or a
value from another block) and after a load_reg it trivializes. ALU ops
are scalar by then otherwise, so ntq_emit_alu only emitted the first
channel of a mov and the other channels of the destination register kept
whatever they held.

It shows with a vec4 phi whose source is not coalesced with the phi web
(a texel fetch in one branch, a value that stays live after the if in the
other): from SSA, the other branch copies the source register into the
phi register through such a vec4 mov, and only .x was copied. A WGSL
fragment shader of the form

   var c = textureLoad(tex_b, p, 0);
   if (u.flag < 0.5) { c = textureLoad(tex_c, p, 0); }
   ... uses of c and of the first fetch ...

read c.yzw from the other branch's (here never written) registers when
the uniform flag was set.

Handle a mov with more than one component like the vecN ops: read every
channel through its swizzle, then move each one.

Tested on a Raspberry Pi 4 (V3D 4.2): the shader above (the blend pass
of a wgpu/Vulkan image editor's compositor) now matches the CPU
reference; that compositor's GPU tests pass 91 of 91 (90 before); the
other GL and Vulkan test programs render unchanged.

Ported unchanged from a Mesa 26.2.x tree used by another operating
system port on the same board (Raspberry Pi 4, V3D 4.2). The numbers
below were measured there, not yet on Phoenix-RTOS.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/broadcom/compiler/nir_to_vir.c | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/broadcom/compiler/nir_to_vir.c b/src/broadcom/compiler/nir_to_vir.c
index e669ff1..063152f 100644
--- a/src/broadcom/compiler/nir_to_vir.c
+++ b/src/broadcom/compiler/nir_to_vir.c
@@ -1381,6 +1381,22 @@ ntq_emit_alu(struct v3d_compile *c, nir_alu_instr *instr)
                 return;
         }
 
+        /* Movs are the other ALU op that can still be a vector here:
+         * nir_trivialize_registers copies whole registers with them (e.g.
+         * a vec4 phi web whose source lives in another register), so move
+         * every channel, not only the first.
+         */
+        if (instr->op == nir_op_mov && instr->def.num_components > 1) {
+                struct qreg srcs[NIR_MAX_VEC_COMPONENTS];
+                for (int i = 0; i < instr->def.num_components; i++)
+                        srcs[i] = ntq_get_src(c, instr->src[0].src,
+                                              instr->src[0].swizzle[i]);
+                for (int i = 0; i < instr->def.num_components; i++)
+                        ntq_store_def(c, &instr->def, i,
+                                      vir_MOV(c, srcs[i]));
+                return;
+        }
+
         /* General case: We can just grab the one used channel per src. */
         assert(nir_op_infos[instr->op].num_inputs <= 3);
         struct qreg src[3] = { 0 };
